  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\n*** MILD START TO THE WEEK, PATCHY FROST(?) MONDAY NIGHT ***\n\nIn the wake of a shortwave trough pivoting from the Great Lakes into \nthe Northeast, mild conditions continue across the local area with \ntemperatures in the low to mid 70s early Sunday afternoon. We'll \nlikely add another 1-3 degrees before cooling into the upper 40s to \nlow 50s tonight. Surface high pressure will move from Illinois to \nOhio tomorrow, settling over the Appalachians tomorrow night. While \nit's to our north during the day, we'll have enough of a gradient to \nfavor a cool northerly breeze around 5-15 mph - confining \ntemperatures to the low-mid 70s - but tomorrow night winds should \nease, particularly over northeastern portions of the area (closer to \nthe high pressure center). With few, if any, clouds around, \nconditions will be favorable for radiational cooling - allowing \ntemperatures to fall into the the low 40s if not upper 30s in the \ntypical cold-prone, low-lying rural areas. If winds really drop off, \nthere may be a field or two that see frost, but NBM suggests \ntemperatures cold enough for this to happen (usually <37 degF) are \nunlikely (less than a 5% chance) for most.\n\n*** SLIGHTLY WARMER, REMAINING DRY INTO SATURDAY ***\n\nWith such a cool start to the day and continued weak easterly flow, \nTuesday will be another seasonably cool day with afternoon highs \ngenerally in the mid 70s, though a few locations over southern \nKentucky may approach 80. We remain south of the main jet stream the \nremainder of the work week, while the dry airmass allows for deep \nmixing each afternoon. As a result, temperatures will warm just a \nsmidgen for Wednesday through Friday; forecast highs are in the low \nto mid 80s (warmest south), while overnight lows will range anywhere \nfrom the mid 40s to mid 50s each day. The only concern during this \nperiod will be worsening and expanding drought conditions across the \nstate, with D1 (moderate drought) observed from Ohio to Monroe \nCounties and points southwest, and D0 (abnormally dry) covering much \nof the rest of the county warning area (CWA) on last weeks' update \nfrom the US Drought Monitor.\n\n*** TROPICAL REMNANTS TO BRING BENEFICIAL RAIN(?) BY SUNDAY ***\n\nLooking ahead to the coming weekend, our attention rests on a \ndisturbance over the southwestern Gulf. The National Hurricane \nCenter (NBC) has given this feature a 30% of development into a \nTropical Cyclone (TC) over the next 7 days, suggesting confidence is \nlow on what will happen. However, if the solution currently depicted \nby both the deterministic GFS and ECMWF (i.e., southwest flow over \nthe Mississippi Valley lifts this feature north into our neck of the \nwoods) materializes, we could see beneficial rainfall by the second \nhalf of the weekend. This is a low confidence scenario, given \nuncertainty about (a) the formation of a tropical system, (b) the \nmovement of that system, and (c) how it interacts with an incoming \ntrough from the west, the timing of which remains unclear per \ncluster analysis. Consequently, member low locations in both GEFS \nand EPS (European Ensemble) vary drastically in timing and track of \nthis tropical feature. However, NBM probabilities for more than 1 \n(2) inches of rainfall for the 48-hour period ending at 8am Monday \nmorning are currently sitting at 30-45% (15-20%) - suggesting \nbeneficial rainfall could aid the developing drought. Stay tuned.",
